Lab 13: Automating MariaDB Backup and Restore
Objectives
By the end of this lab, students will be able to:

Create Ansible playbooks that perform automated MariaDB database backups
Implement automated database restoration procedures using Ansible
Schedule recurring backup operations with cron and validate their execution
Verify backup integrity and confirm successful data restoration
Apply a basic retention policy to manage backup storage
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, students should have:

Basic understanding of Linux command line operations
Familiarity with MariaDB/MySQL database concepts (databases, tables, dump files)
Basic knowledge of Ansible playbooks and YAML syntax
Understanding of cron jobs and task scheduling
Knowledge of file permissions and directory structures
This lab assumes a self-owned or Al Nafi-provided isolated practice environment (CentOS/RHEL 8 or Ubuntu 20.04 LTS) with root or sudo access. All actions are performed against local, disposable test databases created in this lab â�� do not run these playbooks against production systems.

Install the required packages if they are not already present:

# On RHEL/CentOS
sudo dnf install -y mariadb-server mariadb ansible python3-pip

# On Ubuntu/Debian
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y mariadb-server mariadb-client ansible python3-pip

# Required Ansible collection for MySQL/MariaDB modules
ansible-galaxy collection install community.mysql

# Python MySQL client library used by Ansible's community.mysql modules
pip3 install PyMySQL
Task 1: Build an Automated Backup Playbook
Subtask 1.1: Prepare the Lab Environment
Start and enable MariaDB:
sudo systemctl start mariadb
sudo systemctl enable mariadb
sudo systemctl status mariadb --no-pager
You should see active (running) in the status output.

Create the project directory structure:
mkdir -p ~/mariadb-automation/{playbooks,inventory,scripts}
cd ~/mariadb-automation
Create the inventory file:
cat > inventory/hosts << 'EOF'
[database_servers]
localhost ansible_connection=local

[database_servers:vars]
ansible_become=yes
EOF
Secure the root account with a known password (used throughout this lab for consistency):
sudo mysql -u root << 'EOF'
ALTER USER 'root'@'localhost' IDENTIFIED BY 'LabRoot123!';
FLUSH PRIVILEGES;
EOF
Create a credentials file so Ansible and the CLI do not need the password typed interactively:

sudo tee /root/.my.cnf > /dev/null << 'EOF'
[client]
user=root
password=LabRoot123!
EOF
sudo chmod 600 /root/.my.cnf
Deliverable: Running sudo mysql -e "SELECT 1;" succeeds with no password prompt, confirming /root/.my.cnf is being used.

