Subtask 1.3: Create the Backup Playbook
cat > playbooks/mariadb-backup.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: MariaDB Database Backup Automation
  hosts: database_servers
  become: yes
  vars:
    backup_dir: "/opt/mariadb-backups"
    backup_retention_days: 7
    databases_to_backup:
      - company_db
      - inventory_db
    backup_timestamp: "{{ ansible_date_time.year }}{{ ansible_date_time.month }}{{ ansible_date_time.day }}_{{ ansible_date_time.hour }}{{ ansible_date_time.minute }}{{ ansible_date_time.second }}"

  tasks:
    - name: Create base backup directory
      file:
        path: "{{ backup_dir }}"
        state: directory
        mode: '0700'
        owner: root
        group: root

    - name: Create daily backup subdirectory
      file:
        path: "{{ backup_dir }}/{{ ansible_date_time.date }}"
        state: directory
        mode: '0700'
        owner: root
        group: root

    - name: Ensure MariaDB is running
      service:
        name: mariadb
        state: started

    - name: Back up each database individually
      shell: >
        mysqldump --defaults-extra-file=/root/.my.cnf
        --single-transaction --routines --triggers
        {{ item }} | gzip > {{ backup_dir }}/{{ ansible_date_time.date }}/{{ item }}_{{ backup_timestamp }}.sql.gz
      args:
        creates: "{{ backup_dir }}/{{ ansible_date_time.date }}/{{ item }}_{{ backup_timestamp }}.sql.gz"
      loop: "{{ databases_to_backup }}"

    - name: Verify backup files exist and are non-empty
      stat:
        path: "{{ backup_dir }}/{{ ansible_date_time.date }}/{{ item }}_{{ backup_timestamp }}.sql.gz"
      loop: "{{ databases_to_backup }}"
      register: backup_file_stats
      failed_when: not backup_file_stats.stat.exists or backup_file_stats.stat.size == 0

    - name: Record backup completion in log
      lineinfile:
        path: "{{ backup_dir }}/backup.log"
        line: "{{ ansible_date_time.iso8601 }} - Backup completed for: {{ databases_to_backup | join(', ') }}"
        create: yes
        mode: '0600'

    - name: Identify backup directories older than retention period
      find:
        paths: "{{ backup_dir }}"
        age: "{{ backup_retention_days }}d"
        file_type: directory
      register: old_backups

    - name: Remove backup directories beyond retention policy
      file:
        path: "{{ item.path }}"
        state: absent
      loop: "{{ old_backups.files }}"

    - name: Display backup summary
      debug:
        msg: >
          Backup complete. Location: {{ backup_dir }}/{{ ansible_date_time.date }},
          Databases: {{ databases_to_backup | join(', ') }},
          Timestamp: {{ backup_timestamp }}
EOF
Subtask 1.4: Run and Verify the Backup
cd ~/mariadb-automation
ansible-playbook -i inventory/hosts playbooks/mariadb-backup.yml -v
Verify the output files:

ls -la /opt/mariadb-backups/"$(date +%Y-%m-%d)"/
zcat /opt/mariadb-backups/"$(date +%Y-%m-%d)"/company_db_*.sql.gz | head -20
Deliverable: The playbook run ends with failed=0. The daily directory contains two .sql.gz files (company_db_* and inventory_db_*), and zcat ... | head -20 shows a valid mysqldump header beginning with -- MySQL dump.

Task 2: Automate Database Restoration and Validate Integrity
Subtask 2.1: Create the Restore Playbook
cat > playbooks/mariadb-restore.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: MariaDB Database Restore Automation
  hosts: database_servers
  become: yes
  vars:
    backup_dir: "/opt/mariadb-backups"
    restore_date: "{{ restore_target_date | default(ansible_date_time.date) }}"
    databases_to_restore:
      - company_db
      - inventory_db

  tasks:
    - name: Confirm backup directory for restore date exists
      stat:
        path: "{{ backup_dir }}/{{ restore_date }}"
      register: backup_dir_stat

    - name: Fail if backup directory is missing
      fail:
        msg: "No backups found for date {{ restore_date }} in {{ backup_dir }}"
      when: not backup_dir_stat.stat.exists

    - name: Locate latest backup file for each database
      shell: >
        ls -t {{ backup_dir }}/{{ restore_date }}/{{ item }}_*.sql.gz | head -1
      loop: "{{ databases_to_restore }}"
      register: latest_backup_files
      changed_when: false

    - name: Restore each database from its latest backup
      shell: >
        zcat {{ item.stdout }} | mysql --defaults-extra-file=/root/.my.cnf {{ item.item }}
      loop: "{{ latest_backup_files.results }}"

    - name: Record restore completion in log
      lineinfile:
        path: "{{ backup_dir }}/restore.log"
        line: "{{ ansible_date_time.iso8601 }} - Restored databases from {{ restore_date }}: {{ databases_to_restore | join(', ') }}"
        create: yes
        mode: '0600'

    - name: Display restore summary
      debug:
        msg: >
          Restore complete for {{ databases_to_restore | join(', ') }} using
          backups from {{ backup_dir }}/{{ restore_date }}
EOF
Subtask 2.2: Test the Full Backup-Modify-Restore Cycle
Modify data to simulate an incident:
sudo mysql << 'EOF'
USE company_db;
DELETE FROM employees WHERE name = 'John Doe';
INSERT INTO employees (name, department, salary) VALUES ('Accidental Row', 'Testing', 1.00);
SELECT * FROM employees;
EOF
Confirm the table now shows 3 rows: Jane Smith, Mike Johnson, and Accidental Row (John Doe is gone).

Run the restore playbook to bring the database back to its last backed-up state:
ansible-playbook -i inventory/hosts playbooks/mariadb-restore.yml -v
Verify the restoration:
sudo mysql -e "USE company_db; SELECT * FROM employees;"
Deliverable: The employees table shows the original 3 rows (John Doe, Jane Smith, Mike Johnson) with Accidental Row gone, confirming the restore reverted the unwanted changes. The debug task output ends with Restore complete for company_db, inventory_db ....

Subtask 2.3: Verify Backup Integrity Programmatically
Add an integrity-check playbook that confirms every backup file is a valid gzip archive containing a real SQL dump before it is trusted:

cat > playbooks/verify-backups.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Verify MariaDB Backup Integrity
  hosts: database_servers
  become: yes
  vars:
    backup_dir: "/opt/mariadb-backups"
    check_date: "{{ restore_target_date | default(ansible_date_time.date) }}"

  tasks:
    - name: List backup files for the target date
      find:
        paths: "{{ backup_dir }}/{{ check_date }}"
        patterns: "*.sql.gz"
      register: found_backups

    - name: Test gzip integrity of each backup file
      shell: gzip -t {{ item.path }}
      loop: "{{ found_backups.files }}"
      register: gzip_test

    - name: Confirm each archive contains a MySQL dump header
      shell: zcat {{ item.path }} | head -1 | grep -q "MySQL dump"
      loop: "{{ found_backups.files }}"
      register: header_check

    - name: Report integrity check results
      debug:
        msg: "Verified {{ found_backups.files | length }} backup file(s) for {{ check_date }}: all passed gzip and header checks"
EOF
Run it:

ansible-playbook -i inventory/hosts playbooks/verify-backups.yml -v
Deliverable: The play recap shows failed=0. If any file were corrupted, the gzip -t or grep step would fail with a non-zero return code, halting the play.

Task 3: Schedule Automated Backups with Cron
Subtask 3.1: Create a Standalone Backup Script
cat > scripts/mariadb-backup.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
# Automated MariaDB Backup Script

BACKUP_DIR="/opt/mariadb-backups"
DATE=$(date +%Y-%m-%d)
TIMESTAMP=$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S)
DATABASES=("company_db" "inventory_db")
RETENTION_DAYS=7

mkdir -p "$BACKUP_DIR/$DATE"

log_message() {
    echo "$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S') - $1" >> "$BACKUP_DIR/backup.log"
}

log_message "Starting automated backup"

for db in "${DATABASES[@]}"; do
    if mysqldump --defaults-extra-file=/root/.my.cnf --single-transaction --routines --triggers "$db" \
        | gzip > "$BACKUP_DIR/$DATE/${db}_${TIMESTAMP}.sql.gz"; then
        log_message "Successfully backed up $db"
    else
        log_message "ERROR: Failed to back up $db"
        exit 1
    fi
done

log_message "Cleaning up backups older than $RETENTION_DAYS days"
find "$BACKUP_DIR" -maxdepth 1 -type d -mtime +$RETENTION_DAYS -exec rm -rf {} \; 2>/dev/null

log_message "Backup process completed successfully"
EOF
chmod +x scripts/mariadb-backup.sh
sudo cp scripts/mariadb-backup.sh /usr/local/bin/mariadb-backup.sh
sudo chmod 700 /usr/local/bin/mariadb-backup.sh
Subtask 3.2: Schedule the Script with Ansible's Cron Module
cat > playbooks/schedule-backups.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Schedule Automated MariaDB Backups
  hosts: database_servers
  become: yes
  vars:
    backup_script_path: "/usr/local/bin/mariadb-backup.sh"

  tasks:
    - name: Schedule daily backup at 2 AM
      cron:
        name: "MariaDB Daily Backup"
        minute: "0"
        hour: "2"
        job: "{{ backup_script_path }}"
        user: root

    - name: Display scheduling confirmation
      debug:
        msg: "Daily backup scheduled at 02:00 via {{ backup_script_path }}"
EOF
ansible-playbook -i inventory/hosts playbooks/schedule-backups.yml -v
Subtask 3.3: Verify the Scheduled Job and Test the Script Manually
sudo crontab -l
sudo /usr/local/bin/mariadb-backup.sh
tail -5 /opt/mariadb-backups/backup.log
Deliverable: sudo crontab -l lists a line containing 0 2 * * * /usr/local/bin/mariadb-backup.sh. After manually running the script, tail -5 /opt/mariadb-backups/backup.log shows a Backup process completed successfully entry with a fresh timestamp.

Expected Outcomes
A working Ansible playbook that backs up multiple MariaDB databases with compression, verification, and a retention policy
A working Ansible restore playbook that automatically locates and restores the latest backup for each database, validated by a modify-then-restore test
A cron-scheduled backup script confirmed to run automatically and log its results
Troubleshooting
Symptom: mysqldump or mysql commands fail with ERROR 1045 (28000): Access denied for user 'root'@'localhost'. Diagnostic hint: Check that /root/.my.cnf exists, has mode 600, and its password value matches the password you set with ALTER USER. Confirm with sudo cat /root/.my.cnf (as root) and re-test with sudo mysql -e "SELECT CURRENT_USER();".
Symptom: Ansible task fails with Destination directory /opt/mariadb-backups/... does not exist or a Permission denied writing backup files. Diagnostic hint: Verify the playbook is running with become: yes and check ownership/mode with ls -ld /opt/mariadb-backups; the directory should be owned by root with mode 0700 since it holds sensitive dump files.
Symptom: verify-backups.yml fails on gzip -t for a specific file. Diagnostic hint: The backup was likely interrupted mid-write; check backup.log for an error around that timestamp and re-run mariadb-backup.sh manually to regenerate a clean file before trusting it for restore.
Conclusion
In this lab, students built a complete, automated backup and restore workflow for MariaDB using Ansible, including individual database dumps, gzip compression, integrity verification, a time-based retention policy, and cron-based scheduling. Along the way, students practiced core operational concepts: taking consistent backups with --single-transaction, proving restore reliability through a deliberate modify-then-restore test, and validating backup integrity before trusting a file for recovery. Next steps for extending this work include adding monitoring and alerting (for example, notifying an administrator when a scheduled backup job fails or produces a zero-byte file), performing periodic disaster recovery drills on a separate test host to confirm backups are actually usable, and hardening the environment further by encrypting backup archives at rest, restricting backup file access to a dedicated service account instead of root, and storing copies of backups off-host to protect against local disk failure.
